points.explanation:
Remember that {と} can't join adjectives. To say 'cheap **and** tasty', change the first adjective into a linking form:

- {い}-adjectives: drop {い}, add {くて}. {安[やす]い} → {安[やす]くて}
- {な}-adjectives (and nouns): add {で}. {静[しず]か} → {静[しず]かで}

{このパンは安[やす]くておいしいです。} 'This bread is cheap and tasty.'
{あの公園[こうえん]は静[しず]かできれいです。} 'That park is quiet and pretty.'

The tense is decided by the **last** adjective only, so {安[やす]くておいしかったです} means 'was cheap and tasty.' As usual, {いい} becomes {よくて}.

These adverbs go right before the adjective to show degree.

- {とても} = very. Used with positive sentences: {とてもおいしいです。}
- {あまり} = not very. It **must** be used with a negative: {あまりおいしくないです。} 'It's not very tasty.'
- {ぜんぜん} = not at all. Also with a negative: {ぜんぜん寒[さむ]くないです。}

{あまり} is a polite favorite in Japan: saying something is 'not very good' sounds softer than flatly saying it's bad.

You'll also hear {ちょっと} (a little), as in {ちょっと高[たか]いです} 'It's a bit expensive.' That phrase is often a gentle way of saying 'too expensive'.

Hours are number + {時[じ]}. Three hours are irregular, so learn them carefully:

- {四時[よじ]} 4:00 (not {よんじ})
- {七時[しちじ]} 7:00
- {九時[くじ]} 9:00 (not {きゅうじ})

Minutes are number + {分[ふん]}, read {ふん} or {ぷん} depending on the number:

- {ぷん}: {一分[いっぷん]}, {三分[さんぷん]}, {四分[よんぷん]}, {六分[ろっぷん]}, {八分[はっぷん]}, {十分[じゅっぷん]}
- {ふん}: {二分[にふん]}, {五分[ごふん]}, {七分[ななふん]}, {九分[きゅうふん]}

{半[はん]} means 'half past': {三時[さんじ]半[はん]} = 3:30. For a.m. and p.m., put {午前[ごぜん]} or {午後[ごご]} **before** the time: {午後[ごご]二時[にじ]} = 2 p.m.

Ask the time with {今[いま]、何時[なんじ]ですか。}

Japanese picks counters by **shape or type**. Five of the most useful:

- {枚[まい]}: flat things such as paper, tickets, shirts, plates. {一枚[いちまい]}, {二枚[にまい]}... (no sound changes)
- {本[ほん]}: long, thin things such as pens, bottles, umbrellas, trains. {一本[いっぽん]}, {二本[にほん]}, {三本[さんぼん]}, {六本[ろっぽん]}, {八本[はっぽん]}, {十本[じゅっぽん]}
- {冊[さつ]}: books and notebooks. {一冊[いっさつ]}, {八冊[はっさつ]}, {十冊[じゅっさつ]}
- {匹[ひき]}: small animals such as cats, dogs, fish. {一匹[いっぴき]}, {三匹[さんびき]}, {六匹[ろっぴき]}
- {台[だい]}: machines and vehicles such as cars, bikes, computers. {一台[いちだい]}...

Don't panic about the sound changes. If you're unsure, {〜つ} is understood for most objects.

The {て}-form doesn't have a tense on its own, but it's the building block for a huge amount of grammar. Start from the **dictionary form**.

- **Ru-verbs:** drop {る}, add {て}. {食[た]べる} → {食[た]べて}, {見[み]る} → {見[み]て}
- **U-verbs:** the change depends on the last syllable:
- {う・つ・る} → {って}: {買[か]う} → {買[か]って}, {待[ま]つ} → {待[ま]って}, {帰[かえ]る} → {帰[かえ]って}
- {む・ぶ・ぬ} → {んで}: {飲[の]む} → {飲[の]んで}, {遊[あそ]ぶ} → {遊[あそ]んで}, {死[し]ぬ} → {死[し]んで}
- {く} → {いて}: {書[か]く} → {書[か]いて}; {ぐ} → {いで}: {泳[およ]ぐ} → {泳[およ]いで}
- {す} → {して}: {話[はな]す} → {話[はな]して}
- **One exception:** {行[い]く} → {行[い]って} (not {行[い]いて})
- **Irregular:** {する} → {して}, {来[く]る} → {来[き]て}

Many learners memorize the u-verb rules by singing them to a familiar tune. Whatever works for you, practice until it's automatic.

The **dictionary form** is the plain (casual) version of {〜ます}. It's the form you find in dictionaries, and it's the base for lots of grammar like {前[まえ]に}, {つもり} and {とき}, which you'll learn soon.

Going from {ます}-form to dictionary form:

- **Ru-verbs:** replace {ます} with {る}. {食[た]べます} → {食[た]べる}
- **U-verbs:** change the i-sound before {ます} to its u-sound. {飲[の]みます} → {飲[の]む}, {行[い]きます} → {行[い]く}, {帰[かえ]ります} → {帰[かえ]る}
- **Irregular:** {します} → {する}, {来[き]ます} → {来[く]る}

Use it on its own with friends: {明日[あした]、何[なに]する？} 'What are you doing tomorrow?' With casual questions, a rising tone replaces {か}.

The {ない}-form is the plain version of {〜ません}.

- **Ru-verbs:** drop {る}, add {ない}. {食[た]べる} → {食[た]べない}, {見[み]る} → {見[み]ない}
- **U-verbs:** change the final u-sound to its a-sound, then add {ない}. {飲[の]む} → {飲[の]まない}, {行[い]く} → {行[い]かない}, {帰[かえ]る} → {帰[かえ]らない}, {待[ま]つ} → {待[ま]たない}
- **Watch out:** verbs ending in {う} change to {わ}, not {あ}: {買[か]う} → {買[か]わない}, {会[あ]う} → {会[あ]わない}
- **Irregular:** {する} → {しない}, {来[く]る} → {来[こ]ない} (read {こない})
- **Special:** the negative of {ある} is simply {ない}: {お金[かね]がない。} 'I have no money.'

The {ない}-form conjugates like an {い}-adjective, so {〜ないです} is a polite option too.

Use {〜たり〜たりする} to give a few **examples** of activities, without listing everything. It's the verb version of {や}.

Take the plain past ({た}-form), add {り}, and finish with a form of {する}:

- {見[み]た} → {見[み]たり}
- {読[よ]んだ} → {読[よ]んだり}

{休[やす]みの日[ひ]は本[ほん]を読[よ]んだり、音楽[おんがく]を聞[き]いたりします。} 'On my days off, I do things like read books and listen to music.'

The tense and politeness go on the final {する}: {しました} (past), {したいです} (want to). Don't forget it. The sentence isn't finished without {する}.

{でしょう} adds **probability**: 'probably', 'I think', 'I expect'. You'll hear it every day in weather forecasts:

{明日[あした]は雨[あめ]でしょう。} 'It will probably rain tomorrow.'

It attaches to plain forms:

- Nouns and {な}-adjectives directly: {雨[あめ]でしょう}, {静[しず]かでしょう}
- {い}-adjectives: {寒[さむ]いでしょう}
- Verbs in plain form: {来[く]るでしょう}, {来[こ]ないでしょう}

It often comes with {たぶん} (maybe, probably).

With a rising tone, {でしょう？} checks that the listener agrees: 'right?'. In casual speech, {でしょう} becomes {だろう} (when guessing) or {でしょ？} (when checking).
